    problem: "A dental abutment (small threaded post between implant fixture and crown) fatigues and cracks at cyclic bite loads. Currently made from CP Ti Grade 4 (σy 480 MPa). Marketing wants to switch to Ti-6Al-4V ELI (σy 830 MPa, Grade 23). Regulatory wants to know biocompatibility, machinability, and fatigue tradeoffs.",
    hint: "CP Ti and Ti-6Al-4V ELI are both FDA-cleared implantable — the choice is mechanical.",
    solution: "CP Titanium (Commercially Pure): Grades 1-4 (increasing O content). Grade 4 has highest strength of the CPs but is inferior to alloyed Ti. Excellent corrosion resistance, best biocompatibility (single-element passive TiO2), moderate strength. Standard for dental implant fixtures (osseointegration surface).\n\nTi-6Al-4V ELI (Grade 23, Extra-Low Interstitial): 6% Al, 4% V, low O/N/H for improved toughness and fatigue. Twice the strength of Grade 4, higher fatigue endurance (~500 MPa vs ~300 MPa at 10^7 cycles), similar corrosion, similar biocompatibility (aluminum and vanadium ion release has been questioned but current consensus and FDA acceptance is fine for load-bearing implants).\n\nFor a cyclic-loaded small post subject to bite loads (up to 500 N, 10^6-10^7 cycles/year), the fatigue endurance is the deciding factor. Grade 4 at its 300 MPa endurance limit is marginal for the notch stresses at the threaded interface (Kf ~2-3). Ti-6Al-4V ELI provides ~2x margin.\n\nMachinability: both are poor (low k, work hardens), but Ti-6Al-4V is marginally worse. Compensate with high coolant flow, sharp positive rake, and low SFM (100-150 SFM).\n\nSwitch to Grade 23 (ELI is the medical spec — regular Grade 5 is aerospace). Verify with fatigue testing per ISO 14801 (dental implant fatigue standard, ~5x10^6 cycles at max functional load).\n\nCost: Grade 23 is ~1.5-2x Grade 4 raw, similar machining cost. Total abutment cost delta small.\n\nRule: for load-bearing dental/orthopedic implants under fatigue, Ti-6Al-4V ELI is the default. CP Ti Grade 4 is reserved for the bone-integration screw itself (where surface bio > mechanical), covers/plates in low-stress locations, and single-piece plates.",
