    problem: "A machine guard viewing window on a CNC lathe is 6 mm cast acrylic. Within a year: hairline crazing across the surface, opaqueness, and a fracture from a small tool fragment impact. Options: 6 mm polycarbonate, 6 mm PETG, laminated safety glass. Which is right for CNC guarding?",
    hint: "Impact energy from ejected tooling can be several hundred joules; regulatory standards apply.",
    solution: "Acrylic (PMMA): high optical clarity, UV-stable, cheap, but brittle (notched Izod ~ 20 J/m). Attacked by many coolants and solvents (methylene chloride cements it, mineral oils cause crazing under stress). Fracture from a tool fragment is unsurprising — acrylic shatters into sharp pieces.\n\nPolycarbonate (PC): notched Izod 850+ J/m (~40x acrylic). Deforms and stops projectiles rather than shattering. Slightly less clear (~88% vs 92% for acrylic), scratches more easily (Rockwell M 70 vs 92), UV-yellows without stabilizer, attacked by aromatic solvents. Standard for machine guarding.\n\nPETG: intermediate impact (~100 J/m), good chemical resistance, cheap, easy to thermoform. Not rated for high-energy impact guarding.\n\nLaminated safety glass: heavy, precise cut/edge required, expensive, ultimate impact resistance if thick. Overkill for a lathe guard, useful for punch presses.\n\nFor CNC lathe guarding, ANSI B11.19 and EN 12415 specify polycarbonate viewing panels with defined thickness vs spindle energy. Rough rule: 6 mm PC good for small manual lathes; 8-12 mm PC for CNC lathes with 20-40 mm bar capacity; 12 mm PC + PC/PC laminate or PC/glass hybrid for large chuckers.\n\nEnvironmental care: PC crazes if wiped with ammonia-based cleaners or exposed to certain cutting fluid additives (phosphate esters). Use silicone-free, PC-safe cleaners. Replace PC guards every 2-5 years even if visually OK — impact resistance degrades from UV, coolant, and micro-cracking accumulation.\n\nRecommendation: 6 mm polycarbonate as minimum, verify against B11.19 spindle-energy chart. If yellowing/wear is a concern, hard-coated PC (silica-based scratch/UV coating, ~30% cost adder) doubles service life.",
