    problem: "A racing quadcopter arm sees 40g maneuvers, ~500g rotor thrust, 300 mm cantilever length. Currently glass-fiber reinforced polymer (GFRP) at $8/arm. Team wants CFRP at $32/arm for lower flex. Is the mass and stiffness gain worth 4x cost?",
    hint: "Compare specific stiffness (E/ρ), not just E.",
    solution: "GFRP (E-glass/epoxy, ~60% fiber volume): E ~ 40 GPa, ρ ~ 2.0 g/cc, tensile strength ~1000 MPa.\nCFRP (T300 std modulus carbon/epoxy, ~60% fiber): E ~ 130 GPa, ρ ~ 1.55 g/cc, tensile strength ~1500 MPa.\n\nSpecific stiffness (E/ρ): GFRP = 20 GPa·cc/g, CFRP = 84 GPa·cc/g — CFRP is 4x better per unit mass. This is where CFRP earns its cost.\n\nFor a cantilever arm with tip load, tip deflection δ = FL³/(3EI). To match a CFRP arm's stiffness in GFRP, I must increase 3.3x (since E is 3.3x). For a hollow tube of similar OD, that means thicker wall — ~1.5x wall thickness — which multiplies mass ~1.5x on top of the 30% density penalty. Net: GFRP arm to match CFRP stiffness weighs ~2x.\n\nAt racing drone weights (500 g airframe), an extra 20-40 g per arm × 4 arms = 80-160 g of extra mass to lift. Requires more thrust, more current, less flight time, and lowers thrust-to-weight ratio, killing agility.\n\nDamping and failure mode: CFRP is stiffer but less forgiving — fails brittle at high strain. GFRP flexes more, absorbs impact, warps before failing. For crashes (frequent in racing), GFRP arms often bend and recover; CFRP arms snap.\n\nRecommendation: for competitive racing where agility is the metric, CFRP is worth 4x. Design tip deflection < L/500 under max thrust for control fidelity. For hobbyist/practice where crash cost matters, GFRP with sacrificial mounting bolts is cheaper long-term.\n\nRule: CFRP wins on stiffness/mass. GFRP wins on impact tolerance and cost. Match to mission — race or crash.",
