    problem: "An SLS PA12 fixture measures −0.15% in X, correct in Y, +0.05% in Z relative to CAD. Vendor's compensation was set at 3.5% uniform. What's causing the anisotropy?",
    hint: "SLS shrink varies by axis due to sintering direction and thermal history.",
    solution: "SLS PA12 thermal shrink is anisotropic due to build physics:\n- Z (build direction): each layer is added at ~180°C and cools to ~35°C (part cooled after build). Total shrink ~2.5-3%, but new layers are added over already-shrunk lower layers, giving a semi-cumulative effect. Net Z shrink ~2.8-3.2% typical.\n- X and Y (in-plane): shrink 3.3-3.8%, larger than Z because in-plane thermal gradient is stronger (laser scans XY, each pass heats-cools).\n- X vs Y often differ slightly based on scan direction — long-axis scan direction shrinks slightly more due to more thermal history along that axis.\n\nA uniform 3.5% comp works OK on average but leaves per-axis errors:\n- X compensated 3.5%, actual shrink 3.65% → part is 0.15% smaller than target. Matches observation.\n- Y compensated 3.5%, actual shrink 3.5% → dead on. Matches.\n- Z compensated 3.5%, actual shrink 3.45% → part is 0.05% larger than target. Matches.\n\nFix: use axis-specific compensation. Modern SLS software (EOS PSW, 3D Systems Sinter) allows independent XYZ compensation. Set X = 3.65%, Y = 3.50%, Z = 3.45% based on measured coupon data.\n\nCalibration procedure:\n- Print a calibration artifact (cross or brick with features aligned to XYZ, ~150 mm long each axis).\n- Measure with CMM or calipers, compute per-axis shrink vs CAD.\n- Update compensation factors in slicer.\n- Reprint artifact, verify < ±0.1% error per axis.\n- Repeat every material batch (powder chemistry varies slightly) and every 6 months (machine drift).\n\nFor tight-tolerance production (< ±0.15 mm on 100 mm), consider secondary machining pass on critical features. SLS PA12 typical achievable tolerance: ±0.25 mm on first 100 mm, ±0.15% on larger, ±0.5% total for uncalibrated shops.\n\nMJF has similar anisotropy but slightly better repeatability (±0.3 mm typical) due to more uniform thermal input from full-width fusing agent + IR lamp vs. scanning laser.\n\nGeometric distortion: aside from linear shrink, SLS parts also warp — long thin flat parts curl (concave toward heat source). Design ribs and gussets or orient part vertically to minimize.",
